{
  "requirement": "No prompt, tool result, or tool error the harness gives the model MUST state a\nline number or line range of a project source, except within source text it\nquotes and where a tool error repeats a line number the model supplied. The\nharness MUST identify a source location by file and outline selector under\n{cite}`requirements.references.outline_selectors` instead.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Obligations derived from `requirements.authoring.no_line_numbers`**\n1. No tool result may state a line number or range of a project source. Quoted source text is excepted.\n2. No tool error may state one either, except where it repeats a line number the model supplied.\n3. No prompt may state one.\n4. Source locations are identified by file and outline selector, per `requirements.references.outline_selectors`.\n\n**Tool results**\n- `src/authoring/tools.rs#mod[tests]/fn[reading_records_only_delivered_sources_and_their_terms]` checks the `habeas` item-form and line-range results. It asserts that `input` has no `line_start` or `line_end`, that the results give the selector (`fn[work]`), and that recorded inputs equal the file, selector and hash form.\n- `fn[line_range_reads_record_the_innermost_covering_nodes]` covers Rust and plain-text line ranges. It asserts that no returned input or recorded input carries `line_start` or `line_end`, and that items are selectors (`fn[first]`, `mod[inner]/fn[second]`, `line@…`).\n- `fn[search_merges_context_windows_and_respects_ignore_rules]` and `fn[separate_windows_in_one_node_are_divided_by_an_elision_line]` cover `ripgrep` results. They assert no `line_start` on entries and no `line` on each line object. Elision lines carry only text and `matched`.\n- `fn[outline_lists_nested_nodes_without_text_or_inputs]` covers `outline`. It asserts the exact selector/kind/name output and no `line_start`.\n- `fn[references_list_names_and_flattened_sites_without_recording_inputs]` covers `references`. It asserts that sites are exactly name, role, file and item.\n- `src/sites.rs#mod[tests]/fn[sites_are_the_innermost_nodes_of_site_matches_and_violations_within_them]` asserts that the site match JSON given to the model has only kind, query, item and text.\n- `ast_grep` shares the same entry builder as `ripgrep`. The description test asserts its \"without line numbers\" wording, so it needs no separate case.\n\n**Tool errors**\n- `src/authoring/line_numbers.rs#mod[tests]/fn[tool_errors_drop_the_source_line_of_a_parse_error]` asserts the exact rewrite of each error form: a parse-error line prefix, an \"at line, column\" suffix, a `file:N: ` diagnostic prefix, and a file-and-line reference inside a message. Each becomes the `file#` form.\n- The same test asserts that the model-supplied single-line and range forms in the \"is not an inclusive range\" error are repeated unchanged. This is the stated exception.\n- `src/authoring/mod.rs#mod[tests]/fn[tool_errors_give_the_model_no_line_numbers_it_did_not_supply]` is an end-to-end test through `dispatch`. A parse error raised inside the tool contains a line position, but the request delivered to the model does not. A range past EOF repeats only the supplied start line.\n\n**Prompts**\n- `src/authoring/mod.rs#mod[tests]/fn[site_reviews_use_their_prompt_and_record_applies_and_the_site]` compares the whole site-review subject message with the filled approved template. Its matches carry only kind, query and item.\n- `fn[submission_descriptions_explain_arguments_and_successful_completion]` asserts that the submission tool descriptions tell the model to use outline selectors and never line numbers.\n- The mandatory inputs given in prompts are covered by the exact-equality assertions in `src/authoring/inputs.rs#mod[tests]/fn[anchored_files_contribute_root_node_inputs]`. Those inputs are file, item, hash, kind and text only.\n\n**Selector obligation**\nThe exact-output assertions above show locations given as file plus selector, with the `file#` form for root-level diagnostics.\n\n**Result**\nI found no plausible violating behavior that these assertions would accept. No `{fixme}` acknowledgments apply, and none are needed.",
  "references": "5f07d59d3d70ca6a503110d2a797133c2b9f1eafd54fb53e7ee2fb02587ec51a",
